to-runar-bjarnason-source : List Word
to-runar-bjarnason-source = lines-of (cons "# Rúnar Bjarnason"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Rúnar, your work has run on one idea for over a decade: a program is better described than executed. Every piece of your work that hyperactivity builds on is yours, and you have full credit for it. This page sets out what that work is, what in it reaches further than the field has noticed, and what hyperactivity does with it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## The idea underneath the work"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The field reads your 2012 Scala Days paper, *Stackless Scala with Free Monads*, as a fix for a JVM limitation. That is its smallest reading. Its content is that a `Trampoline` is `Free[Function0, A]`, and that a free monad models any recursive data type with data at the leaves. A computation's control stack becomes an ordinary value, and one loop interprets it. Once control is data, it can be inspected, transformed, stored and moved."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Your keynote *Constraints Liberate, Liberties Constrain* said the general form. A component that promises less can be composed more freely; a component that can do anything can be reasoned about not at all. The field took this as a style maxim for API design. It is a statement about composition, and it holds at every scale where things compose."
  (cons ""
  (cons "*Functional Programming in Scala*, with Paul Chiusano, taught a generation that algebra is how programs are designed: laws first, implementations as models of the laws."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Unison is the same idea applied to code itself. Paul, Arya Irani and you identified each definition by the hash of its syntax tree, which makes names metadata, and that one constraint frees code from builds, from dependency conflicts and from the gap between a running system and its source. Abilities extend the free-monad discipline to effects: a computation states what it asks for, and a handler decides what that means. You maintain the standard library that makes this ordinary, and Unison Cloud runs it: statically typed, dynamically deployed, in the words of your 2024 YOW! talk. You built the company as a public benefit corporation so the commercial platform funds the open language."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity takes up"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Hyperactivity is in Avik Jain's words a language and “a computational model of reality i.e. wolfram's ruliad executing on silicon”, with cubical type theory at its core"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ". Its paper names Unison as the precedent for content-addressed definitions: a definition is stored with its typed syntax tree and dependency references, and a name is a view"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p2 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". Its source has no files: the hash is the reference, and the names log records who pointed which name at which content, and when"
  (cons (cite metacircular-univalent-unison-of-cubically-receptive-stateboxes-source "(Part III, Unison)")
  (cons ""
  (cons ""
  (cons "**The free monad is in the paper.** One representation of terminating processes in hyperactivity is a Church-encoded free monad over a command signature"
  (cons (cite s7-6-p4 "(§7.6)")
  (cons ". A handler supplies the interpretation of requests in a particular world, and different handlers yield different observations of the same process description"
  (cons (cite s10-p6 "(§10)")
  (cons ". Your construction is the paper's construction."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Control as data.** The language operates on a representation of its own terms"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p2 "(§6.2)")
  (cons ". A suspended computation keeps its residual expression, its dependency observations, its pending request and the continuations waiting on it"
  (cons (cite s10-1-p5 "(§10.1)")
  (cons ". Specialization returns a new code with a code for its justification"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p6 "(§6.2)")
  (cons ". The stack is a representation, never a constraint."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What univalence adds"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The paper separates three statements: equal digests, definitional equality, and a specified relation between meanings"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p4 "(10.22)")
  (cons "; a digest identifies a representation and does not prove semantic equivalence"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p3 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". Univalence turns an equivalence into an identification, and transport along it computes"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p5 "(§1.1)")
  (cons "; operations and their laws are transported together across a structural equivalence"
  (cons (cite s4-p2 "(§4)")
  (cons ". The store records each relation with its evidence and uses the strongest available one"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p5 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". A refactoring becomes a relation between two hashes held with its proof, and what depends on one carries to the other."
  (cons ""
  (cons "This is your own thesis at its strongest. The constraint is that every construction respects equality. Its freedom is that whatever is proved of one representation carries to every equivalent one."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What becomes of the interpreter"
  (cons ""
  (cons "You showed that a program can be a data structure whose meaning is supplied by an interpreter. The paper follows this to observation itself: a reading determines the family of histories that could have produced it"
  (cons (cite s1-p8 "(1.1)")
  (cons ", a conclusion is supported exactly when it holds over that whole family"
  (cons (cite s1-p13 "(§1)")
  (cons ", and where several possibilities remain they are retained rather than collapsed by assumption"
  (cons (cite s1-p14 "(§1)")
  (cons ". Handlers in Unison choose an interpretation; hyperactivity makes the family of interpretations an object of the language."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Why this matters to you"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Unison made a correct bet early and has borne its cost. Univalence does not change that bet; it extends it. Code addressed by content, with equality as data, is a model in which every tool you built is still the right one. Avik wants to join forces with Unison on exactly this. The work credits you, Paul and Arya in full for content-addressed code, for abilities as the effect discipline, and for the free-monad and trampoline constructions. Read the rest of the graph looking for where it serves Unison."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- About the Unison team, https://www.unison-lang.org/about-us/"
  (cons "- Why Unison Computing is a public benefit corporation, https://www.unison-lang.org/blog/benefit-corp-report/"
  (cons "- Rúnar Bjarnason at YOW! Brisbane 2024, https://yowcon.com/brisbane-2024/speakers/3656/runar-bjarnason"
  (cons "- Stackless Scala with Free Monads (notes with links to the Scala Days 2012 paper), https://www.eed3si9n.com/herding-cats/stackless-scala-with-free-monads.html"
  (cons "- Conference report, Scala World 2015: Constraints Liberate, Liberties Constrain, https://www.inkl.com/news/conference-report-scala-world-2015"
  (cons "- Design Principles From Functional Programming with Rúnar Bjarnason, Software Engineering Daily, https://softwareengineeringdaily.com/2018/01/30/design-principles-from-functional-programming-with-runar-bjarnason/"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Parts I and III, the definitions linked above"
  nil)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
